keel (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A monitor section of the spec, handbook decision 0021 (keel#43):
    thresholds for the disks, inodes, memory, swap, CPU, load per core
    and declared network interfaces, and the channels to tell: email to
    security.alerts' address, Telegram, ntfy and a Slack compatible
    webhook. Defaults as decided: disk 80/90, inodes 90, memory 85 for 5
    minutes, swap 50 for 5, CPU 90 for 10, load 2 per core for 10.
    Enabled without a working channel is a validation error, and so is
    email while security.alerts is skip; URLs are https, or a secret
    file holding one, and are never repeated in a message; tokens are
    secret files.
  * apply --system writes /etc/monit/conf.d/keel.conf, mode 0600: one
    check per filesystem that holds data (no pseudo, network or FUSE
    filesystem), warn and critical as separate services, a reminder and
    a recovery on every test, each throughput direction its own. The
    cycle is monit's, read from monitrc and its includes and never set,
    and a for_minutes monit cannot hold in 64 of those cycles is
    refused. On the live system monit -t and a reload, only when the
    file changed; refused when monit is not installed, since keel
    installs no package, and when a keel.conf keel did not write is
    there. Turned off, the files keel wrote are removed.
  * apply --system writes /etc/keel/monitor.json, mode 0600: the
    channels resolved from the spec, token files by path, which is all
    keel notify reads. An unusable token file is a note, not a failure.
  * keel notify, which keel.conf runs: one message, with what to do, to
    every channel in /etc/keel/monitor.json, never the spec, refused
    unless root owns it and nobody else can use it, as secret files are.
    Standard library HTTPS with verification, no redirect followed, no
    token in argv, output or a URL it prints. details: false leaves the
    directory and process lists out; one du per filesystem at a time.
    When no channel takes it, syslog at user.crit and root's mailbox,
    and exit code 22, NOTIFY_FAILED.
  * inspect reads the monitor section back from keel.conf at monit's
    cycle, and diff compares it; the channels are neither compared nor
    repeated, JSON included.
  * Suggests: monit.

