keel (0.3.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * An origin and the host pattern MariaDB holds for it are one
    authorization. docs/spec.md tells an operator to prefer a prefix and
    records that MariaDB can only spell an IPv6 prefix as
    2804:710:d0:5:%, so keel diff now compares the two as the same value
    instead of reporting drift on every primary whose description used the
    preferred form. keel.spec.origins is the one place the two spellings
    meet: canonical() for the comparison, host_pattern() for the grant the
    apply phase writes.
  * A pattern that stops inside a group, and one that names a wider range
    than the description asked for, are drift as before.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 13:20:00 +0000

keel (0.3.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel diff compares the database section. The engine, the role and the
    listen addresses are compared against what the server says it is; the
    endpoint a replica replicates from and the origins a primary authorizes
    are compared when the declared role has a use for them; and the
    credentials, being references whose values are never read, are not
    compared at all.
  * A declared replica against an observed primary is drift that carries a
    warning saying never to correct it automatically, because demoting a
    primary destroys everything written to it since the replica last agreed.
    The mirror image, a declared primary against an observed replica, carries
    its own: promoting splits the pair into two writable servers. docs/diff.md
    states this as a rule of the project, for the phase that will configure
    replication and not only read it. The warning is in the JSON document as
    well, so a caller shows it without reproducing it.
  * A field the declared role has no use for is not compared: a standalone may
    carry the authorizations it will need as a primary, the way a certificate
    configuration waits behind tls.acme.enabled. The role itself is always
    compared. Where the declared and observed roles disagree, the role line
    carries the drift and the field it governs is unknown, naming the role the
    machine is in, so one fact produces one drift line.
  * Listen addresses and authorized origins compare as sets and in canonical
    form. A name is never resolved: an authorization that names a host and a
    server that holds an address are two different things, and resolving
    before comparing is how a name that stopped resolving would pass as same.
  * inspect says why a field of another role was not read, so the reason
    reaches the diff line.
  * MariaDB is asked for its replica status without --skip-column-names.
    SHOW REPLICA STATUS\G prints one "Name: value" per line and that flag
    takes the names away, so the primary's address arrived as a bare line and
    the reading found no primary on a machine that was plainly replicating.
    Found on the bench, which is why the bench is part of this issue: the
    fixtures agreed with the code. The role no longer depends on a value
    happening to contain a colon either; any output at all is a replica row,
    because SHOW REPLICA STATUS prints nothing on a server that replicates
    from nowhere.
  * allowed_from accepts a host pattern, 2001:db8:1:%, which is the only
    spelling MariaDB has for an IPv6 prefix in a grant: it takes an address and
    a netmask for IPv4 alone. Found on the bench, on a primary authorized from
    a /64, where inspect read the pattern off the server and the description it
    wrote did not validate. A test now asserts that every origin the reading
    can produce is one the schema accepts, on both SQL engines.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 11:05:00 +0000

keel (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * keel inspect reads the database section, both subjects independently. The
    server side asks the server what it is rather than reading a configuration
    file, because a file that reads correctly is what shipped the PostgreSQL
    appliance listening on IPv4 only: MariaDB is asked for its replica status,
    its connected replicas and the Host of every account that may replicate;
    PostgreSQL for pg_is_in_recovery(), pg_stat_replication,
    pg_stat_wal_receiver and its pg_hba rules for the replication pseudo
    database, through pg_hba_file_rules, which is the server's own parse and
    not the file; Redis for INFO replication, INFO cluster and INFO server.
  * An engine counts as installed when its server binary is present, not when
    its configuration directory is, because mysql-common puts /etc/mysql on a
    machine that holds the client alone.
  * A machine in a mode the description has no role for, a Galera node or a
    Redis Cluster node, is reported as a role that could not be inferred,
    naming the mode. So is a server that is installed and cannot be asked: an
    offline root, a stopped service, or a Redis with requirepass set, which
    refuses redis-cli and which inspect will not read a secret to get past.
    The whole section is not inferred in that case, so every field of it is
    unknown to keel diff and none of it is drift.
  * database.server.listen comes from the listening sockets, filtered on the
    port the server itself reported, so it is what the machine answers on and
    not what it was told to answer on.
  * The client side is read from the application's own configuration, which is
    the one place that fact lives: wp-config.php and NodeBB's config.json, one
    reader per application in a table that grows as appliances arrive. No
    password is read from any of them.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 10:20:00 +0000

keel (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The instance description gains a database section with two subjects, which
    one field cannot serve: database.server is what a machine that runs a
    database server is, a role of standalone, primary or replica; and
    database.client is where a machine that uses a database reaches it, a
    primary for writes and optionally replicas for reads. A machine can
    declare both, either or neither. MariaDB, PostgreSQL and Redis are the
    three engines. Nothing is applied: apply warns that the section was left
    alone, with or without --system.
  * Every address in the section is checked as a literal address and the word
    localhost is refused by name, with the reason in the message: Debian maps
    ::1 to ip6-localhost and never to localhost, which is how the PostgreSQL
    appliance shipped listening on IPv4 only. ip6-localhost and ip6-loopback
    are refused the same way.
  * A primary authorizes rather than enumerates. In asynchronous replication
    the replica opens the connection and the primary holds no list of who is
    replicating, so database.server.replication.allowed_from is a list of
    origins that may replicate: a prefix, which is the form to prefer with
    IPv6 and no NAT, a single address, or a name, which is accepted and is
    fragile because both MariaDB grants and pg_hba resolve names and fail
    quietly.
  * The replication and client credentials are file references like every
    other secret, and docs/spec.md says what the value unlocks per engine,
    because Redis has requirepass and ACL users rather than a database
    account.
  * keel.spec.validate_secret holds the check of one secret reference, which
    was in keel.spec.validate, so a section validator can use it without
    importing the validator that calls it.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 09:40:00 +0000

keel (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The system phase keeps one entry for the host in /etc/hosts. A line that
    names the host without a fully qualified name, the 127.0.1.1 <name> line
    09hostname leaves, is replaced wherever it stands and not only at the
    address being written: a resolver answers from the first line that
    carries the name, so the short line answered first and hostname -f kept
    answering the short name although the file held the fully qualified one.
  * The reader of /etc/hosts stops at the first line that names the host, the
    way a resolver does. An entry another line answers before is no longer
    reported by inspect, is no longer called same by diff, and is converged by
    the system phase instead of being called settled. Proved on a container
    booted from the published core layer: hostname -f answered the short name
    on such a file, and the phase turned it into one entry.
  * An unknown fully qualified name says which command writes it.

 -- KeelLinux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 22:55:00 +0000

keel (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The package now ships a first boot hook,
    /usr/lib/inithooks/firstboot.d/10keel-system, which converges the system
    state the instance description declares by running "keel spec apply
    --system-only". It is numbered to run after 09hostname, whose sed over
    the old name would otherwise edit or lose the /etc/hosts entry, and
    before the hooks at 15, 29 and 30 that depend on the fully qualified
    name. The package that owns the keel command owns the hook that runs it,
    so inithooks needs no dependency on keel and an image without keel has
    no hook there. The hook is a no-op with exit 0 where there is no
    description, under turnkey-init, and where the description declares
    nothing that phase converges; it logs through the inithooks log and
    never aborts a boot for a failure it cannot fix.
  * keel spec apply gains --system-only, the system phase without the conf
    phase: no secret is resolved, so a generated password the earlier hooks
    already applied is never regenerated.

